# No incidental reformatting

When you edit a file, change only the lines the task needs. Do not fix indentation, re-wrap lines, re-sort import blocks, or clean up pre-existing lint in the same change — even when the fix is correct. Every such hunk is one more thing the reviewer has to read to find the real change.

```python
# BAD — the task was to add `role=` on one line; the diff also re-sorts an
# import block that was already there and re-wraps a neighbouring call.
-from amenity_enrichment import (LlmUsage, parse_room_categories,
-    amenity_llm_clients)
+from amenity_enrichment import (
+    LlmUsage,
+    amenity_llm_clients,
+    parse_room_categories,
+)
-    other_client.call(a, b,
-        c)
+    other_client.call(a, b, c)
     usage.add_chat(response.usage, role="merge_judge", model=self.model)

# GOOD — only the line the task needed changed. Wrapping *that* line to fit
# the file's width is fine; it is yours.
-    usage.add_chat(response.usage)
+    usage.add_chat(
+        response.usage, role="classify_amenity_or_rule", model=self.classify_model
+    )
```

This narrows the Ruff rule: run Ruff on files you touched, and fix what *you introduced* (an unused import you added, an import you added out of order). If Ruff also flags something that was already there, leave it and say so. `ruff check --fix` on a whole file re-sorts every import block in it — run it only when the file's imports are yours, or apply the fix to your lines by hand.

Formatting, indentation and lint clean-ups are welcome — on their own branch, as their own PR, so they can be reviewed as "no behaviour change" in one pass.
